  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nGOES-East upper level water vapor imagery shows the key \nfeatures that will be important for the longer term forecast. A \nbroad cyclonic spin is along the central West Coast associated \nwith a longwave trough. Ahead of this is remnant moisture from \nOdalys, which is being drawn northeastward over the Four Corners\nregion already. And finally, Hurricane Polo is readily apparent\noff the southern portion of Baja California as it begins its \nnorth and later northeastward track and weakens. By Tuesday \nmorning, this trough will be over the Southwest with the \nweakening Polo cresting the Sierra Madre Occidental. There will \nalready be pieces of energy spilling northeastward into our \nregion along with surges of low level thermal lift and modest QG\nconvergence to support showers and storms spreading into at \nleast some portions of Iowa later Tuesday if not Tuesday night. \n\nThere is uncertainty on where the highest rainfall of this long \nduration rain will set up over the region. AI guidance has shown \npromise for highlighting the location of the band of heaviest \nprecipitation and over the last day, the AIFS has stabilized in the \n48 hour rainfall total axis through midday Thursday. Meanwhile, the \nAIGFS continues to trend northward in its placement. Looking at \ndeterministic and ensembles, these have generally trended northward \nin the last several runs as well. These shifts compared to 24 hours \nago do not lend themselves to forecast confidence, but investigating \nsome of the causes for these shifts does provide elements of the \nweather pattern to monitor this week. First, what degree of \ninteraction will the approaching trough and tropical moisture have \nwith a northern stream wave over the Canadian prairies. Yesterday's \n12z suites had minimal interaction, which resulted in the northward \nsurging moisture being met with dry air in the mid-levels. Overnight \nand so far today, models are showing more play with the northern \nstream and thus a change of rainfall being farther northward and \noriented from southwest to northeast vs west to east. Second, a zone \nof high pressure over the upper Mississippi River Valley at the \nstart of this week will slide eastward and sprawl over the Eastern \nSeaboard. This may leave some degree of mid-level dry air over the \nregion, though uncertainty is where and how long that may last \nagainst the northward push of moisture. Third and finally, last \nnight's and today's GFS and CMC have a northern stream trough \nadvancing slower than at least last night's ECMWF, which allows the \nGFS and CMC to pick up the approaching trough and take it through \nthe state more quickly. However, the ECMWF is faster with the \nnorthern trough, which causes the approaching trough to break off \ninto a closed low. Thus, the rainfall does not progress through as \nfast, but in the end the closed low does pull it away from the state \nby late week. Depending on where this rain falls will have \nimplications on river flooding and possible flash flooding, which \nare also highlighted in this morning's National Water Center's \nhydrologic discussion (NMDNWC).\n\nWhile the details are more murky beyond Tuesday night in location of \nhighest rainfall, confidence is high that it will be raining over \nsome portions of Iowa from late Tuesday through Wednesday night. In \naddition, the rain that does fall will be in an environment that \nwill support efficient rainfall given the atypical deep warm cloud \ndepths and above 90th percentile precipitable water values. This \nwill lead to areas of rainfall of 2 inches or more somewhere in Iowa \nby week's end. 7z/13z NBM probabilities of 2\"+ rainfall in a 48 hour \nperiod ending Thursday morning are already above 50% over much of \nwestern and central Iowa with 4\"/48 hour probabilities between 20 \nand 30% over parts of western Iowa. And ending on a bit of good \nnews, the weak lapse rates will minimize any severe weather risk \nthis week.",
